  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PhotoTextContentData
  data:
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Introduction

'
    description: "Cable is the medium through which information usually moves from
      one network device to another. \r\n\r\nThere are several types of cables which
      are commonly used with LANs. In some cases, a network will utilize only one
      type of cable, while other networks will use a variety of cable types.\r\n\r\nThe
      type of cable chosen for a network is related to the network's topology, protocol,
      and size. \r\n\r\nUnderstanding the characteristics of different types of cable
      and how they relate to other aspects of a network is necessary for the development
      of a successful network\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: Network Media
    description: "The network can be connected through two types of connections:
      wired and wireless.\r\n\r\nHaving a wired connection is more reliable than
      a wireless connection. However, wireless connections are widely used by some
      devices such as cellphones and laptops for their portability.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe
      following are different wired connections commonly used in networking. However
      for this Qualification, we are focusing on UTP and STP Cables which are most
      common for Local Area Networks.\r\n\r\n1. Unshielded Twisted Pair (UTP) Cable
      - A popular type of cable that is commonly used for Local Area Networks. It
      has a two shielded wires twisted around each other.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n2.
      Shielded Twisted Pair (STP) Cable - The extra covering of the STP Cable provides
      extra protection to prevent electromagnetic interference.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n3.
      Coaxial Cable - This the cable commonly used in television industries. The
      metal shield surrounding the cable helps block interference from fluorescence
      lights, motors, and other computers. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n4. Fiber
      Optic Cable - Transmits light rather than electronic signals which reduce the
      risks of electrical interference. It is ideal for environments where electrical
      interference is present.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Wireless Connections

'
    description: "Wireless connections make use of LANs which allow high frequency
      radio signals to communicate between work stations, servers, or hubs. \r\n\r\nWireless
      networks are ideal for allowing laptops, portable devices, and other devices
      to connect to the LAN. This is also beneficial in establishments where it may
      be harder to install cables. \r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Wireless Networking

'
    description: 'A wireless network is any type of computer network that use wireless
      data connections for connecting network nodes.

      

      Wireless PAN
      - Stands for Wireless Personal Area Networks (WPANs). This connection interconnect
      devices within a small area. An example would be bluetooth and infrared.

      

      Wireless
      LAN - Known as WLANs. This is used to connect to local resources and the internet.
      It links two or more devices over a short distance using a wireless distribution
      method - usually through an wireless router/wireless access point for internet
      access. Wi-Fi falls under WLANs.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Wireless Mesh Networks

'
    description: 'This is a wireless network made up of radio nodes organized in
      a mesh topology. It can be a

      Wireless MAN or Wireless Metropolitan
      Area Network which connects several WLANs.

      Wireless WAN or Wireless
      Wide Area Network which covers large areas such as cities/towns, branch offices,
      business, public internet access system, etc.

      Global Area Network (GAN)
      for used for mobile technology such as texting, calling, etc.

      Space
      Network which is used between spacecrafts within the vicinity of earth. NASA''s
      Space Network makes use of this technology.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Cable Installation

'
    description: 'In network installation, we have to be familiar with the RJ45 (Registered
      Jack).

      

      

      

      

      

      It is a jack used
      for Ethernet networking and used for cables. Both T568A and T568B are common
      wiring standards however the T568B is widely used in the Philippines.

      

      There
      are two common network cabling namely the straight-through and the crossover
      cables.

      

      A straight-through cable is also known as the patch
      cable or Ethernet cable. This is the most common format for network cables.
      The wires have same pins from either end.  This is used when connecting a computer
      to a modem, router, or switch. This is also used between networking equipment
      that are not similar to each other.

      

      A crossover cable crosses
      the wires from the other end differently to the other. We use these when we
      connect two devices of the same type like two computers or two similar networking
      devices. These wirings use two different ends, T568A and T568B.

      

      However,
      if we use a device between two devices (such as a switch), all cables are straight
      through.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: Ethernet Cable Fabrication Procedure
    description: '1. Prepare Materials: Gather all the necessary tools and materials
      needed for the fabrication.

      RJ45

      Cat5 UTP Cable

      Crimping
      Tools

      Wire Stripper

      Side Cutting Plier

      LAN Tester

      2. 
      Decide whether you are making a T568B Straight-Through or a Crossover cable.
      Take note of the specific color coding sequence for your chosen cable type
      before starting.

      3. Measure and Cut: Pull out 1 meter of Cat 5 UTP
      (Unshielded Twisted Pair) cable.

      4.  Using a cable stripper, peel approximately
      1 inch of the outer jacket off the UTP cable to expose the inner twisted wire
      pairs.

      5. Untwist the pairs and arrange the individual wires into the
      proper color-coding sequence according to your table (Straight-Through or Crossover).

      6.
      Trim the ends of the arranged wires evenly to ensure they are all exactly the
      same length.

      7.  Carefully slide the aligned wires into the RJ45 connector,
      making sure each wire goes all the way into its correct slot.

      

      8. 
      Insert the RJ45 plug into the crimping tool and squeeze firmly to lock the
      pins onto the wires.

      9.  Repeat the stripping, arranging, trimming,
      and crimping process for both ends of the cable.

      10. Once the cable
      is complete, plug both ends into a LAN tester to check your work:

      

      For
      Straight-Through Cables: The lights on both sides of the tester should match
      up in parallel. They must light up sequentially in the exact same order from
      1 to 8.

      

      For Crossover Cables: The lights on the receiving
      end will not match the sequence of the transmitting end (which goes 1 to 8),
      except for pins 4, 5, 7, and 8, which will remain the same.

'
    has3dView: 0
